way is actively moving crates from the Portland wharf into a powered underground refrigeration vault—and finds her father's original 1998 binder open to a passenger-and-specimen log from a ship that supposedly sank 26 years ago.2. MASTER CHRONOLOGICAL TIMELINEERA 1: The Barrow Island Origin & Calder Maritime (1984 – ~2018)1984: Rhode Island decommissions Quarantine Station No. 4 (B.I.Q.S.-04), a nineteenth-century brick contagious-disease hospital and morgue on Barrow Island in lower Narragansett Bay. The island is sold off to a private Providence maritime shell trust.1998:A vessel supposedly sinks off Block Island.Calder Maritime & Cargo (C.M.C.), a three-man marine surveying firm on Custom House Wharf in Portland, Maine, run by Tessa and Nina's father, audits the initial offload at Barrow Island (C.M.C. — AUDIT 1998).Six refrigerated steel containment crates are offloaded into the decommissioned basement morgue of Ward C on Barrow Island (B.I.Q.S.-04 — WARD C), along with a bonded passenger-and-specimen transfer log from the "sunken" Block Island vessel.The 1998 offload sheet is countersigned by Victor Garroway (later Chief Marine Underwriter at Harbor Mutual Insurance).The calfskin ledger used to track the off-book escrow disbursements is stamped on its back cover with the steel notary seal: QUARANTINE STATION NO. 4 — BARROW ISLAND, R.I. and C.M.C. — AUDIT 1998.Tessa's Sophomore Year of College (~2014–2016):Tessa and Nina's father dies suddenly of a coronary. Calder Maritime & Cargo folds.Tessa keeps her father's estate boxes, brass parallel ruler, and cracked leather logbook in the spare room of her Portland apartment, unaware that Nina later found the missing 1998 carbon sheet and the WARD C brass key among his papers.ERA 2: The CutlerCor Trial Deaths & Nina's Disappearance (August – October 2020)August – October 2020:21-year-old Nina Calder works an internship in a Burlington, Vermont biostatistics office auditing clinical trials for Cutler Biotech, owned by A